The error happens because `posts` starts out as `undefined` (since you didn't pass an initial value to `useState()`). When the component renders the very first time, it tries to run `.map()` on `undefined`, which crashes the app. 

Additionally, the code is missing `async/await` (or proper error handling/loading states) which is best practice for data fetching.

Here is the fixed component:

```jsx
import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';

export default function Posts() {
  // 1. Initialize `posts` as an empty array instead of undefined
  const [posts, setPosts] = useState([]);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);
  const [error, setError] = useState(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    async function fetchPosts() {
      try {
        const response = await fetch('https://api.example.com/posts');

        if (!response.ok) {
          throw new Error('Failed to fetch posts');
        }

        const data = await response.json();
        setPosts(data);
      } catch (err) {
        setError(err.message);
      } finally {
        setLoading(false);
      }
    }

    fetchPosts();
  }, []);

  // 2. Handle loading and error states gracefully
  if (loading) return <p>Loading posts...</p>;
  if (error) return <p>Error: {error}</p>;

  // 3. Now it's safe to map because posts is guaranteed to be an array
  return (
    <ul>
      {posts.map(p => (
        <li key={p.id}>{p.title}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}
```

### What changed?
1. **`useState([])`**: Initialized the state as an empty array. Even before the API finishes loading, `posts.map()` will just loop over 0 items and render nothing, preventing the crash.
2. **Loading & Error States**: Added checks so the user knows what's happening while the network request is in flight.

